2. The Long-Form Blog Font Formula

For long-form reading (1,500+ words), you need fonts that have excellent
"x-height" (the height of the lowercase 'x', which determines legibility at
smaller sizes) and clear differentiation between similar letters (like 'l', 'I',
and '1').

Here are the two industry-leading recommendations for the LoveQNA blog:

Option A: The Modern & Clean Approach (Highly Recommended)

This combination is used by many modern publishing platforms because it is
crisp, fast to read, and highly accessible.

  - Headings: Merriweather (Bold)
      - Why: Merriweather is a serif font designed specifically for screens. Its
        bold weights are strong and authoritative without being heavy, and it
        pairs beautifully with a sans-serif body text. It feels warm and
        traditional but renders sharply.
  - Body Text: Lato (Regular)
      - Why: Lato is a humanist sans-serif. It is incredibly clean and legible,
        with a slightly rounded structure that feels friendly and
        empathetic—perfect for relationship advice. It remains comfortable to
        read even after scrolling for thousands of words.

Option B: The Classic & Timeless Approach

This mimics the feel of a high-quality book.

  - Headings: Playfair Display (Bold)
      - Why: High contrast, elegant, and authoritative. It adds a touch of
        sophistication to your advice columns.
  - Body Text: Lora (Regular)
      - Why: Lora is a contemporary serif optimized for body text. It is rooted
        in calligraphy but designed for digital environments, providing a warm,
        intellectual, and highly readable experience for long-form essays.

My Vote for LoveQNA: I recommend Option A (Merriweather & Lato). It bridges the
gap between "wise" (Merriweather) and "approachable" (Lato) most effectively.

3. Image Borders & Dividers: The Verdict

You asked about using White or Brown for image borders and dividers on the blog
page.

Verdict: Use a warm, dark brown (#3B251A) that complements your burgundy accent,
but avoid pure white for dividers.

Here is the reasoning:

  - The Case Against White Dividers: A pure white horizontal rule () or border
    on a cream (#FFF8E7) background will look like a mistake or a glitch. It
    lacks contrast and will just make the background look dirty.
  - The Case for Dark Brown Borders: A thin, subtle border in a color like
    #3B251A (a deep, desaturated espresso brown) will look sophisticated, warm,
    and intentional. It frames images without the harshness of a black border.
    It ties into the "relationship/growth" theme nicely by feeling organic and
    earthy.
  - The Case for White/Off-White Image Backgrounds: If you are embedding a photo
    of people, placing that photo inside a strict, thin #3B251A border works
    well.
      - Alternatively, if the photo has a white background (like a headshot),
        you can place it directly on the cream page. If the photo does not have
        a white background, you should first place the image onto a pure white
        canvas, and then put a thin #3B251A border around that entire white
        block. This makes photos "pop" cleanly without clashing with the cream
        page.

The Divider Formula:

Do not use lines. For long-form blogs, empty space (whitespace) is the best
divider.

Between major sections, instead of a line, use 60px to 80px of top-margin
(padding). This allows the eye to rest and makes the long text less
intimidating.

If you must use a visual divider for thematic shifts (e.g., moving from "The
Problem" to "The Solution"), use an ornamental element in your Accent Color
(#501A1A), perhaps a very small, centered icon, like an elegant heart or
asterisk, rather than a plain line.

This branding pattern gives LoveQNA a professional, empathetic, and highly
readable identity.
